@*
    One status word, read against the vocabulary the surface allows.

    The read-only half of a record's status: the word, the tone the vocabulary
    gives it, and a flag when the vocabulary has never heard of it. The select the
    headline offers instead is the other half, and both take their modifier from
    the same MetadataStatusVocabulary — built twice they drifted, which is the
    reason there is one answer here rather than two callers each working it out.

    Composed from Badge rather than assembled here, so there is one implementation
    of what a badge's classes are. This adds exactly three things Badge cannot
    know: which modifier the word maps onto, whether the word is one the surface
    allows, and what to say when it is not.

    Nothing here judges a word without a vocabulary. MetadataStatusVocabulary.None
    is the default and flags nothing: a surface that named no vocabulary has not
    claimed that anything is wrong, and the same word means different things in
    different places, so a verdict guessed without one would be wrong as often as
    right.

    Nothing in, nothing out: a block that states no status should not leave an
    empty badge on the line.

    KnowledgeStatusPill is this component with a knowledge folder's vocabulary
    already looked up. It was the only shape of this that existed, and it carried
    the folder lookup inside it, which is what kept MetadataView tied to
    KnowledgeFolder. The pill stays — the folder is a real thing a knowledge
    surface has — and delegates here.
*@

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Status))
{
    <Badge Kind="status"
           Slug="@Vocabulary.SlugFor(Status)"
           Title="@Vocabulary.Expectation(Status)"
           CssClass="@ExtraClasses"
           TestId="@TestId"
           @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">@Status</Badge>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The status word, exactly as it was authored. Never normalised:
    /// the flag exists to make a typo visible, and a badge that tidied the word
    /// up would hide the thing it is flagging.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The words the surface allows, and how each is drawn. The default
    /// shows the status and judges nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataStatusVocabulary Vocabulary { get; set; } = MetadataStatusVocabulary.None;

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The flag, then the caller's own class. Badge appends this whole
    /// string after the badge classes, and the caller's part goes last within it,
    /// so nothing a caller passes displaces a class the stylesheet or a test
    /// matches on by exact position.
    ///
    /// <para>The flag's name is still <c>knowledge-status--unrecognised</c>. It is
    /// the hook the stylesheet already has and the one every test matches on, and
    /// renaming a CSS class is a change of its own rather than part of taking the
    /// folder out of the record.</para></summary>
    private string? ExtraClasses
    {
        get
        {
            var flag = Vocabulary.IsUnrecognised(Status) ? "knowledge-status--unrecognised" : string.Empty;

            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass)) return flag.Length == 0 ? null : flag;

            return flag.Length == 0 ? CssClass : $"{flag} {CssClass}";
        }
    }
}
